## 1. SETTLED PREMISES -- none carried from an earlier campaign (first brief).  What follows was READ from HEAD today
## (f4f601c4) or MEASURED today; each item says which.

* **Who installs the device** (read): `src/machines/machine_test.c` adds `fbctrl addr=0x12f00000` in
  `default_test()` -- called by testarm, testm88k, testppc, testriscv and testsh -- and again in testmips's own
  setup; unconditionally in each.  The bare* machines do not.  No other file instantiates it.
* **The register map** (read, `src/include/testmachine/dev_fb.h`, verbatim):

```
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_ADDRESS		0x12f00000
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_LENGTH		      0x20


/*
 *  First choose the port by writing the port index to DEV_FBCTRL_PORT,
 *  then read or write DEV_FBCTRL_DATA.
 */

#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT			      0x00
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_DATA			      0x10

#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_COMMAND			0
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_X1			1
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_Y1			2
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_X2			3
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_Y2			4
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_COLOR_R			5
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_COLOR_G			6
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_COLOR_B			7
#define	DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS		8
```

* **The device state and init** (read, `src/devices/dev_fbctrl.c`, verbatim).  `DEVINIT` zeroes the struct, so
  `current_port` starts at 0; the only other store to `current_port` in the tree is the PORT arm below
  (`grep -rn current_port src/` -- check it).

```
struct fbctrl_data {
	struct vfb_data *vfb_data;
	int		current_port;
	int		port[DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS];
};
...
DEVINIT(fbctrl)
{
	struct fbctrl_data *d;

	CHECK_ALLOCATION(d = (struct fbctrl_data *) malloc(sizeof(struct fbctrl_data)));
	memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct fbctrl_data));

	d->vfb_data = dev_fb_init(devinit->machine, devinit->machine->memory,
	    DEV_FB_ADDRESS, VFB_GENERIC, 640, 480, 640, DEV_FBCTRL_MAXY(640),
	    24, "generic");

	memory_device_register(devinit->machine->memory, devinit->name,
	    devinit->addr, DEV_FBCTRL_LENGTH, dev_fbctrl_access, d,
	    DM_DEFAULT, NULL);

	return 1;
}
```

* **THE WHOLE ACCESS FUNCTION, verbatim** (every exit path and the common tail; `idata` is `uint64_t`, `current_port` is
  `int`):

```
DEVICE_ACCESS(fbctrl)
{
	struct fbctrl_data *d = (struct fbctrl_data *) extra;
	uint64_t idata = 0, odata = 0;

	if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)
	        idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len);

	switch (relative_addr) {

	case DEV_FBCTRL_PORT:
		if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
			odata = d->current_port;
		else {
			d->current_port = idata;
			if (idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS)
				fatal("[ WARNING: fbctrl port number is out"
				    " of range! ]\n");
		}
		break;

	case DEV_FBCTRL_DATA:
		if (d->current_port < 0 || d->current_port
		    >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS) {
			fatal("[ fbctrl port number is out of range! ]\n");
			exit(1);
		}

		if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
			odata = d->port[d->current_port];
		else {
			d->port[d->current_port] = idata;
			if (d->current_port == DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_COMMAND)
				fbctrl_command(cpu, d);
		}

		break;

	default:
		fatal("[ dev_fbctrl: unimplemented relative addr 0x%x ]\n",
		    (int)relative_addr);
		exit(1);
	}

	if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
		memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata);

	return 1;
}
```

* **The command helper the DATA arm calls, verbatim** (needed for the device-signature control below, and it holds a
  second process-ending call that is OUT OF SCOPE here -- see section 4):

```
static void fbctrl_command(struct cpu *cpu, struct fbctrl_data *d)
{
	int cmd = d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_COMMAND];
	int x1, y1, i;

	switch (cmd) {

	case DEV_FBCTRL_COMMAND_NOP:
		break;

	case DEV_FBCTRL_COMMAND_SET_RESOLUTION:
		/*
		 *  Change the framebuffer resolution to X1 x Y1 pixels.
		 */
		x1 = d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_X1];
		y1 = d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_Y1];
		debug("[ dev_fbctrl: changing resolution to %i,%i ]\n", x1, y1);

		dev_fb_resize(d->vfb_data, x1, y1);

		/*  Remember to invalidate all translations for anyone
		    who might have used the old framebuffer:  */
		for (i = 0; i < cpu->machine->ncpus; i++)
			cpu->machine->cpus[i]->invalidate_translation_caches(
			    cpu->machine->cpus[i], 0, INVALIDATE_ALL);
		break;

	case DEV_FBCTRL_COMMAND_GET_RESOLUTION:
		if (cpu->machine->x11_md.in_use) {
			d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_X1] = d->vfb_data->xsize;
			d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_Y1] = d->vfb_data->ysize;
		} else {
			// When not using X11 output, return 0x0 size.
			d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_X1] = 0;
			d->port[DEV_FBCTRL_PORT_Y1] = 0;
		}
		break;

	/*  TODO: Block copy and fill.  */

	default:fatal("fbctrl_command: Unimplemented command %i.\n", cmd);
		exit(1);
	}
}
```

* **THE DEFECT, as I read it.**  The PORT arm STORES any value with only a warning -- `d->current_port = idata;` runs
  first, the range check after it only prints.  The DATA arm then tests the stored `int` and calls `exit(1)` when it is
  out of range.  So two ordinary guest accesses -- a store of 8 or more to `+0x00`, then any access to `+0x10` -- end
  the emulator process.  The two guards also test DIFFERENT QUANTITIES: PORT tests the 64-bit `idata`, DATA tests the
  `int` it was truncated into.  Enumerated for the values a guest store can produce:

      guest store to +0x00          idata                 PORT arm today       current_port   DATA arm today
      sw 5                          0x5                   stored               5              serviced
      sw 7                          0x7                   stored               7              serviced
      sw 8                          0x8                   WARNING, stored      8              exit(1)
      sw 9                          0x9                   WARNING, stored      9              exit(1)
      sw -1 (0xffffffff)            0xffffffff            WARNING, stored      -1             exit(1)
      sd 0x0000000100000003         0x100000003           WARNING, stored      3 (truncated)  serviced as port 3
      sb 0xff                       0xff                  WARNING, stored      255            exit(1)

  The `sd` row is the interesting one: the PORT arm calls the value out of range and the model then uses port 3.  (The
  int conversion of an out-of-range `uint64_t` is implementation-defined in C99; 3 is what gcc on x86-64 gives.  Check
  whether `memory_readmax64()` really delivers all 8 bytes for an `sd` on `-E testmips` -- I believe it does, and the
  `sd` row depends on it.)

* **THE WITNESS -- rung 3, MEASURED today, test-first, on HEAD's binary** (`build/gxemul`, built 06:38 from sources that
  `cmp` clean against the repo; `dev_fbctrl.c` unchanged since the upstream import).  Unmodified `-E testmips -M 64`,
  started at the debugger, real guest `sw`/`lw` instructions placed with `put w` and executed one `step` at a time, so
  every access goes through real address decode and `memory_rw`.  `t0 = 0xffffffffb2f00000` (kseg1 of 0x12f00000).
  Arm K: `sw t1(=9),0(t0)` then `lw t2,16(t0)`.  Arm L (the controls): PORT=5, DATA=0x2a, read DATA and PORT back
  (LIVENESS); then X1 (port 1) = 123, COMMAND (port 0) = 2 (GET_RESOLUTION), read X1 (DEVICE SIGNATURE: the model zeroes
  X1 without X11; a RAM cell would read back 123).  Every stepped word is compared with the emulator's own step line.
  The output, verbatim:

```
HEAD=f4f601c4  binary=build/gxemul md5=9fa0c1f9 mtime=2026-09-24 06:38:35  dev_fbctrl.c: cmp build == SEC, binary -nt source
  arm K  HOST-DIED steps=1 exit=1 warn=True killdiag=True
      t2 = -
  arm L  SURVIVED  steps=10 exit=0 warn=False killdiag=False
      t4 = 0x2a
      t5 = 0x5
      s2 = 0x0
CONTROLS
  ok   L survived every step                                      SURVIVED steps=10
  ok   L every stepped word disassembled as planted               0 mismatches
  ok   L LIVENESS: DATA round-trips 0x2a                          t4=42
  ok   L LIVENESS: PORT reads back 5                              t5=5
  ok   L SIGNATURE: X1 zeroed by GET_RESOLUTION (RAM: 123)        s2=0
SYMPTOM (arm K)
  ok   K every stepped word disassembled as planted               0 mismatches
FBCTRLW_CONTROL=OK
FBCTRLW_K=HOST-DIED-ON-DATA exit=1
FBCTRLW_DONE
```

  `steps=1` on arm K means the `sw` completed and the `lw` did not: the process ended on the DATA access, exit status 1,
  after printing both the PORT warning and the DATA arm's own diagnostic.  The row requires the diagnostic as well as the
  death, so a death for another reason would not count.

## 2. THE PROPOSAL -- a sketch, to be argued with

**The fix (option-set member 3, decline + latched announce, at the PORT arm).**  Refuse the out-of-range selector:
`current_port` keeps its previous, always-valid value, and the refusal is announced ONCE per device instance with the
house `(once)` marker (the tree's idiom is a per-instance latch field; `dev_disk.c` in the same test-machine family uses
function-static `#119` loud-once latches, which are global rather than per-device):

```
 struct fbctrl_data {
 	struct vfb_data *vfb_data;
 	int		current_port;
 	int		port[DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS];
+	int		warned_port;	/* #549: the out-of-range PORT announce, once */
 };
 ...
 	case DEV_FBCTRL_PORT:
 		if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
 			odata = d->current_port;
-		else {
-			d->current_port = idata;
-			if (idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS)
-				fatal("[ WARNING: fbctrl port number is out"
-				    " of range! ]\n");
-		}
+		else if (idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS) {
+			/*  #549: REFUSED -- current_port keeps its previous, valid value.
+			    Storing the selector let the next DATA access end the process. */
+			if (!d->warned_port) {
+				fatal("[ fbctrl: port number 0x%llx is out of range; the "
+				    "selector is unchanged (once) ]\n",
+				    (unsigned long long) idata);
+				d->warned_port = 1;
+			}
+		} else
+			d->current_port = idata;
 		break;
```

The DATA arm is NOT edited: once PORT refuses, `current_port` is always in [0, 7], so its guard can no longer fire and
stays as belt-and-braces -- a future edit that breaks the PORT refusal ends the process loudly instead of indexing
`port[]` out of bounds.  **That choice is the round's main design question (Q3).**  `est/` gets the byte-identical file;
the three build trees are rebuilt and cleared.

**The detector (ships with the fix): `regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py`, grown from the witness**, same session plumbing,
every row on a fresh `-E testmips` unless noted:

* the controls of arm L, unchanged (liveness, device signature, disassembly of every stepped word);
* **refusal, anchored**: PORT=5, DATA=0x2a, then PORT=9 -- the process survives, DATA still reads 0x2a (port 5's cell,
  not port 0's), PORT reads 5;
* **boundary**: PORT=8 refused (PORT still reads the previous 5); PORT=7 accepted (reads 7) and DATA=0x77 round-trips
  (the far bound, written at the LAST legal slot); PORT=0 accepted (the near bound);
* **wide**: `sd 0x0000000100000003` to PORT is refused (PORT still reads 5) -- the truncation cell in the table above;
* **negative**: `sw -1` refused;
* **announce**: exactly ONE `(once)` line after three refused writes on one device; ZERO in the controls-only arm;
* **pre-fix red**: on HEAD's binary the refusal rows end the process (the witness above).

**Its home.**  No gate drives a test machine's devices today.  Three gates start `-E testmips`: `gate_asan_sweep.sh`
(gate 9) only CONSTRUCTS it, `gate_upstream.sh` runs upstream unit tests on it, and `regress/gate_mips_folds.sh`
(gate 16) is the one that DRIVES a testmips guest at the debugger (`mips_fold_probe.py`) -- so the sketch adds the
probe there, with the same
`gatedevicesplit` note `#548` wrote into gate 14 (device probes accumulating in CPU gates is an OWED split).  The probe
must also be registered where `tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py` looks, or it lands as an unwired probe.

## 3. QUESTIONS

1. **Premise audit.**  (a) Is the PORT arm really the only writer of `current_port` besides `DEVINIT`'s `memset`?
   (b) Once PORT refuses, can ANY sequence of guest accesses still reach the DATA arm's `exit(1)` -- any width, any
   offset inside the 0x20-byte window, any byte lane of a big- or little-endian guest?  Enumerate what reaches `case
   DEV_FBCTRL_PORT` and with what `idata`.  (c) Is the `sd` row real on `-E testmips` (a 64-bit CPU by default), and
   does `memory_readmax64()` deliver the full 8 bytes?  (d) Does anything in the tree depend on the WARNING text I am
   replacing?
2. **What is the SMALLEST EDIT to the fix that breaks it and still passes every row proposed above?**  Name the row you
   expect to stay green.  Consider the predicate (`>=`, the operand, its width or sign), the latch (set too early, never
   set, shared), and the store (reached anyway, reached with a clamped value, reached on some widths only).
3. **The DATA arm: keep its `exit(1)`, turn it into a decline, or both?**  Argue it from what each choice does to a
   future edit that breaks the PORT refusal, and from what the detector could then observe.  Is a process-ending call
   the guest can no longer reach acceptable in this fork, or is there a precedent for converting such guards anyway?
4. **Option-set completeness.**  Members: 1 delete, 2 silent decline, 3 decline + latched announce, 4 accept, 5 fault
   (a bus error -- MIPS and M88K deliver one for a failed device access; ARM, PPC, SH, RISC-V test machines also carry
   this device), 6 backed accept, 7 implement.  Is 3 at PORT right, and is there a member or an in-tree idiom this brief
   did not name?  Should the refusal keep the previous selector, or reset it to 0, or clamp?
5. **The detector's home and wiring** -- gate 16 with a note, `gate_mips.sh`, or elsewhere?  What does
   `check_probe_wiring.py` require of a new probe, and does anything else count probes (a manifest, a pin)?
6. **Scope.**  Section 4 lists the two other process-ending calls in this file.  Is it right to leave them to their own
   rounds, or do they meet the reopening rule (same device, same semantic class, same detector, no new oracle)?

If, while verifying a claim, you found something ELSE -- say so.

## 4. OUT OF SCOPE HERE, AND WHERE IT GOES

`dev_fbctrl.c` has two more calls that end the process, both in the text above: `fbctrl_command()`'s `default:` --
reached by ANY command other than 0-2, **including the documented `DEV_FBCTRL_COMMAND_FILL` (3) and `COPY` (4), which
the source marks `TODO`** -- and `DEVICE_ACCESS`'s `default:` for any offset in the 0x20-byte window other than 0x00
and 0x10 (for example a 1-byte access to +0x03, the low byte of PORT on a big-endian guest).  They are different
classes (an unimplemented command value; an unmapped offset), so this round names and files them rather than taking
them.  The tracking row `exitunowned76` lists `fbctrlportkill` among six one-to-five-line fixes; the other two sites
will get their own rows.

