202:  the emulator process.  The two guards also test DIFFERENT QUANTITIES: PORT tests the 64-bit `idata`, DATA tests the
203:  `int` it was truncated into.  Enumerated for the values a guest store can produce:
204:
205:      guest store to +0x00          idata                 PORT arm today       current_port   DATA arm today
206:      sw 5                          0x5                   stored               5              serviced
207:      sw 7                          0x7                   stored               7              serviced
208:      sw 8                          0x8                   WARNING, stored      8              exit(1)
209:      sw 9                          0x9                   WARNING, stored      9              exit(1)
210:      sw -1 (0xffffffff)            0xffffffff            WARNING, stored      -1             exit(1)
211:      sd 0x0000000100000003         0x100000003           WARNING, stored      3 (truncated)  serviced as port 3
212:      sb 0xff                       0xff                  WARNING, stored      255            exit(1)
213:
214:  The `sd` row is the interesting one: the PORT arm calls the value out of range and the model then uses port 3.  (The
215:  int conversion of an out-of-range `uint64_t` is implementation-defined in C99; 3 is what gcc on x86-64 gives.  Check
216:  whether `memory_readmax64()` really delivers all 8 bytes for an `sd` on `-E testmips` -- I believe it does, and the
217:  `sd` row depends on it.)
218:
219:* **THE WITNESS -- rung 3, MEASURED today, test-first, on HEAD's binary** (`build/gxemul`, built 06:38 from sources that
220:  `cmp` clean against the repo; `dev_fbctrl.c` unchanged since the upstream import).  Unmodified `-E testmips -M 64`,
221:  started at the debugger, real guest `sw`/`lw` instructions placed with `put w` and executed one `step` at a time, so
222:  every access goes through real address decode and `memory_rw`.  `t0 = 0xffffffffb2f00000` (kseg1 of 0x12f00000).
223:  Arm K: `sw t1(=9),0(t0)` then `lw t2,16(t0)`.  Arm L (the controls): PORT=5, DATA=0x2a, read DATA and PORT back
224:  (LIVENESS); then X1 (port 1) = 123, COMMAND (port 0) = 2 (GET_RESOLUTION), read X1 (DEVICE SIGNATURE: the model zeroes
225:  X1 without X11; a RAM cell would read back 123).  Every stepped word is compared with the emulator's own step line.
226:  The output, verbatim:
